// @run
// @log 3
// @log 3
// @log 6
// @info `acc` is never reassigned; use `const` instead of `let`
// DECLARED, NOT ACTED ON: `let` is the filed witness's spelling and the fixture keeps it
// verbatim. The lint is right and the ablation says so — `const acc` trapped identically
// before the fix, so the storage class was never an ingredient (D1513).
// A CHAIN OF MODULE GLOBALS THAT REFERENCE EACH OTHER, read back through an expression that
// mentions two of them more than once — the shape that TRAPPED the compiler (D1513). Nothing
// here is exotic: `globalCellKind`'s ~40-rung ladder re-enters itself through the `expr*`
// predicates, so every rung re-derived the whole chain and one added link MULTIPLIED the work
// instead of adding to it. Seven lines exhausted the 4 GiB GC heap; the fix is the memo on
// that ladder, and this file is what keeps the shape compiled.
//
// IT IS A COST FIXTURE WEARING AN OUTPUT ASSERTION, which is the only kind this harness can
// carry: there is no wall-clock budget here (`tests/vl_scaling_shape_test.ts` owns ratios).
// What it pins is that the chain COMPILES AT ALL — before the fix `vl check` returned 0 and
// then the seed died inside itself.

// L1 — the filed witness: an inferred-empty inner list, the nesting, a bound element read and
// a three-term expression naming both the bound read and a direct index.
const xs0 = []
xs0.push({ y: 1, x: 2 })
const outer = [xs0]
const inner = outer[0]
const first = inner[0]
let acc = 0
print(first.y + inner[0].x + acc)

// L2 — the same chain one link LONGER, through plain aliases. Each alias multiplied the cost
// before the fix (four of them was already past the ceiling); they must all be free now.
const a1 = outer
const a2 = a1
const a3 = a2
const a4 = a3
const inner2 = a4[0]
const first2 = inner2[0]
print(first2.y + inner2[0].x)

// L3 — the EXPRESSION axis, which multiplied independently of the chain: every reference to a
// chained global paid a fresh walk, so a longer `+` chain over the same two globals was the
// second way past the ceiling.
print(first.y + inner[0].x + first.x + inner[0].y)
